ach other, our clients and the market. Our culture is non-hierarchical and one where everyone is valued. We strive for excellence in everything we do.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Role&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Shared Engineering is a mixed-discipline platform team of around a dozen engineers (network engineers, Linux engineers, and software developers). The team owns the foundations that the firm&#39;s engineering runs on such as alerting, job scheduling, host inventory/monitoring, and the internal developer tooling for our monorepo. This includes shared libraries, build systems and CI/CD infrastructure. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Our aim is to give the firm&#39;s engineers leverage: fewer manual steps, delivery that is fast and predictable, and teams who can self-serve rather than raising a ticket and waiting. We have many initiatives in flight at once, so judgement about which thing to work on matters nearly as much as building it well. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;This is a software-developer role on the team focused on developer experience and platform software: building the tools, services, and libraries that make everyone else&#39;s engineering faster and more reliable. A growing part of that remit is how the firm&#39;s engineers adopt AI and coding-assistant tooling. Recent work includes our self-service network configuration tools, sandboxing and gateway services for coding assistants, and pulling host inventory data into a single source of truth. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Responsibilities&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Working primarily in Go, build and own platform services and internal tooling end-to-end, from design through to production.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Improve developer experience by reducing friction in the build/CI/CD path, strengthen shared libraries.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Help shape how the wider firm adopts AI/coding-assistant tooling safely and effectively, integrating it into workflows and building guardrails and tooling around it.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Collaborate with the network and Linux engineers to understand what the infrastructure needs in order to scale and be managed, then expose it cleanly in software.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Mentor and share knowledge, improving teammates&#39; skills and making sure others can maintain and build on the systems you develop.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Review and raise standards by thoughtful code review, sensible judgement on where to block versus where to let things flow, and a pragmatic sense of the right work to be doing right now.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Take part in on-call once up-to-speed, troubleshooting problems that span several technical domains across current and legacy infrastructure.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Essential attributes&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;3+ years&#39; of experience, with strong software-engineering fundamentals and production experience owning services end-to-end. Go is what you&#39;d write day to day, but we care more about the fundamentals than the language: strong backgrounds in other statically typed languages are welcome.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Comfortable on Linux, with an understanding of the OS your software runs on (processes, networking basics, packaging, systemd), even if you&#39;re not a systems engineer.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Solid grasp of build systems, CI/CD, and release pipelines. You&#39;ve worked in a monorepo or a serious build/release setup and made it better.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A track record of delivering: shipping, closing out, and not letting scope or polish stall a deliverable.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Good written and verbal communication, and the collaborative instinct to treat non-developer engineers as first-class colleagues.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Desirable attributes &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;Things we&#39;d like to see. You won&#39;t have all of them, and that&#39;s fine.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Interest or hands-on experience in developer experience and AI/coding-assistant tooling. Building or integrating LLMs into a real engineering workflow is a plus, but curiosity about how these tools change work practices counts too.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Experience with infrastructure/platform software: inventory/asset systems, monitoring, alerting, scheduling, container orchestration or similar backend platform work.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;APIs, data pipelines and schema management: designing clean service APIs and moving data between systems reliably.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Infrastructure-as-code and cloud fluency. We use Terraform and AWS as required, but experience with Azure or GCP is just as relevant.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The role is not managing hardware, networks or datacenters. However, you will collaborate closely with teams that do, so experience in these areas is valuable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

